[;binomial_coefficients
 { binom(n,k) = n C k
  = frac(n!, (n-k)! k!) = frac(falling_factorial(n,k), k!) }
 Ground rule: normal cases are {0 <= i <= n}
 We know {n C 1 = n}
 Well what about 2 {n C 2 = (n C 1)(n-1 C 1) / 2 = n(n-1) / 2}
 Finally we have 3 {n C 3 = n(n-1)(n-2) / 3!}
 
 Also we have this "theoretical" way of writing it:
 
 where {falling_factorial(n,k) = frac(n!, (n-k)!)}
 
 [;Pascals_rule
  It allows you to "decompose" by {n}
  {(n C k) + (n C k+1) = n+1 C k+1}
  or, if you wanna break down a larger choice
  {n C k = (n-1 C k-1) + (n-1 C k)}
  [#done How to remember why that's true?
   [Choosing {k=3} items out of {n=6} items out of the list
    {a b c d e f}
    Can be broken down into a sum of these two:
    1. The groups of 3 with {a} in them: there are {5 C 2} of them: {b c}, {b d} {e f}, etc.
    2. The groups of 3 without {a} in them: there are {5 C 3} of them {b c d}, {b d e}, etc.
    ]
   [NOTE It's still not at all easy to remember, but at least that's an interpretation]
   ]
  [#done Proof
   {(n C k) + (n C k+1)
    = (n! / ((n-k)!    k!) +
       n! / ((n-k-1)! (k+1)!))
    = (n! (k+1) / ((n-k)! (k+1)!) +
       n! (n-k) / ((n-k)! (k+1)!))
    = (n+1)! / (((n+1)-(k+1))! (k+1)!)
    = n+1 C k+1
   }]
  ]
 
 [#done Wait, how do we get {n C 0 = 1}?
  So if we follow @Pascals_rule, then
  {n C 1 = (n-1 C 0) + (n-1 C 1)
   -> n  = (n-1 C 0) + (n-1)
   -> n-1 C 0 = 1
  }]
 
 [#done What about {n C -1 = 0}?
  {n C 0 = (n-1 C -1) + (n-1 C 0)
   -> 1 = (n-1 C -1) + 1
   -> (n-1 C -1) = 0
  }]
 
 [What about {n C n+1 = 0}?
  {(n C n) + (n C n+1) = n+1 C n+1
   -> 1 + (n C n+1) = 1
   -> (n C n+1) = 0  // #qed
  }]
 
 [NOTE People don't realize,
  but there are a bunch of zeros outside of the Pascal triangle;
  |NOTE (You can add to the top, see the Mathematica page)
  | 0 0 1 0
  |0 0 1 1 0
  | 0 1 2 1 0
  |0 1 3 3 1 0
  ]
 
 [;binomial_shift
  We can decrement both {n} and {i} like this
  { binom(n,i) = n/i binom(n-1, i-1) }
  [Proof
   {
    i binom(n,i)
    = i n! / ((n-i)! i!)
    = n (n-1)! / (((n-1)-(i-1))! (i-1)!)
    = n binom(n-1, i-1)
   }
   ]
  ]
 ]

[;binomial_theorem
 {(a+b)^n
  = sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) a^i b^(n-i)
  = (n C 0) b^n + (n C 1) a b^(n-1) +...+ (n C n) a^n b
 }
 
 This can be proven by an informal argument:
 In general, {a^i} appears {n C i} times in the expansion, because
 {(a+b)^n = (a+b)(a+b)^(n-1)
  = a(a+b)^(n-1) + b(a+b)^(n-1)}
 
 {a^(i-1)} appears {n-1 C i-1} times in {(a+b)^(n-1)},
 and {a^i} appears {n-1 C i} times in {(a+b)^(n-1)}
 so in total, {a^i} appears {(n-1 C i-1) + (n-1 C i) = n C i} times
 
 And the entire expansion is just a collection of these, so #qed.
 ]

[;binomial_chain_rule
 { binom(n,k) binom(k,j)
  = binom(n,j) binom(n-j, k-j)
  = binom(n,k-j) binom(n-k+j, j)
 }
 proven by algebra
 ]

[Relation of entries on the same row
 { binom(n,k) - binom(n,k-1) = frac(n+1-2 k, n+1) binom(n+1, k) }
 proven by algebra
 ]

[{ binom(n,k) = frac(n+1-k, n+1) binom(n+1,k) }
 proven by algebra, algebra always work!
 ]
